+
+Summary of the final run: lib tests `106 passed` (includes the state machine),
+`hand_written` `50 passed; 2 failed; 4 ignored` (the 4 ignored are pre-existing
+`#[ignore]` tests), xml fixture tests `4421 passed`, doctests `5 passed`. The two
+failures are stable and reproduce identically from the failure database.
+
+Per the skill's "Run and Reflect" step:
+- **Liveness check:** temporarily inverting the min/max clamp property and the model
+  invariant produced shrunk counterexamples (all-zero draws / three `new_leaf` steps
+  respectively), then the changes were reverted.
+- **Exploratory high-count run:** the whole hegel suite was run once with
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10x). No additional failures appeared; the two failing tests
+  shrank to the same minimal counterexamples. Committed tests are back at the default.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+Both failures were verified to be **upstream taffy 0.12.2 behaviour**: the checked-out
+`src/` is byte-identical to the `taffy-0.12.2` crate from crates.io (verified with
+`diff -r` against the registry sources), and both reproduce in ~10-line standalone
+programs with no hegel involvement.
+
+### 1. `TaffyTree::remove` does not invalidate the parent's layout cache
+
+- **Failing test:** `hegel_incremental_relayout_matches_fresh_tree`
+- **Minimal counterexample (as shrunk by hegel):** root with default (Flex) style and a
+  single child whose only non-default style is `border.bottom: length(1.0)`; available
+  space `MinContent × MinContent`; mutation `op = 1` (remove the child, `idx_1 = 0`).
+  After `remove(child)` + `compute_layout`, the root's layout is
+  `size: 0.0 × 1.0` while a fresh tree with the same structure computes `0.0 × 0.0`.
+- **Standalone repro:** build the tree, `compute_layout(root, Size::MIN_CONTENT)`,
+  `taffy.remove(child)`, `compute_layout` again → root size is still `0×1`, and
+  `taffy.dirty(root)` returns `false` immediately after `remove`.
+- **Why it's a library bug:** every other mutating method (`add_child`,
+  `insert_child_at_index`, `remove_child_at_index`, `remove_children_range`,
+  `replace_child_at_index`, `set_children`, `set_style`, `set_node_context`) calls
+  `self.mark_dirty(...)`; `remove` is the only one that detaches a node from its parent
+  without invalidating the parent chain, so the next `compute_layout` serves the stale
+  cached result (`compute_cached_layout` hits the root's `final_layout_entry`).
+- **Code path at fault:** `TaffyTree::remove`, `src/tree/taffy_tree.rs` lines 618–638 —
+  it does `children.retain(|f| *f != node)` on the parent but never calls
+  `self.mark_dirty(parent)`.
+
+### 2. A hidden child's `Layout::order` depends on cache state (incremental vs fresh divergence)
+
+- **Failing test:** `hegel_relayout_after_mark_dirty_is_stable`
+- **Minimal counterexample (as shrunk by hegel):** root (default Flex style) with two
+  default-style leaf children where the second has `display: None`; available space
+  `MinContent × MinContent`; `mark_dirty` on the *first* (visible) child. After
+  recomputing, the hidden child's `Layout.order` changes from `0` to `1` — nothing
+  else differs.
+- **Standalone repro:** first layout → hidden child `order == 0`;
+  `mark_dirty(visible_sibling)` + relayout → hidden child `order == 1`.
+- **Why it's a library bug:** the layout of an unchanged tree must not depend on which
+  caches happen to be warm; the same tree yields different `Layout` output for the
+  hidden node on the first computation vs a cached recomputation (and hence incremental
+  layout diverges from from-scratch layout).
+- **Code path at fault:** interplay of three pieces:
+  - `compute_flexbox_layout`'s hidden-children pass (`src/compute/flexbox.rs` ~line
+    394-407) first writes `Layout::with_order(order as u32)` (the child index) and then
+    calls `perform_child_layout`;
+  - on an uncached run this reaches `compute_hidden_layout` (`src/compute/mod.rs` line
+    277-292), which overwrites the layout with `Layout::with_order(0)` — so a fresh
+    layout always ends with `order == 0`;
+  - but the hidden child's result was stored in the cache under
+    `RunMode::PerformLayout` (`Cache::store`, `src/tree/cache.rs`), and
+    `mark_dirty(sibling)` only clears the sibling-and-ancestor chain. On the second run
+    `compute_cached_layout` (`src/compute/mod.rs`) returns the cached output, the
+    `compute_hidden_layout` side-effect is skipped, and the `with_order(index)` written
+    by the flexbox pass survives.
+
+  Either the flexbox/grid/block hidden-pass `with_order(order)` write or the
+  `compute_hidden_layout` `with_order(0)` write is the intended final value; today which
+  one you observe depends on cache warmth. (Cosmetic in impact — the node is hidden —
+  but it makes incremental layout non-reproducible, which is exactly what this property
+  exists to catch.)
+
+Both tests are left in place, failing, per instructions.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **"Children lie within their parent's bounds."** Considered (it was suggested as a
+  promising angle) but not implemented as a general property: CSS/taffy layout
+  legitimately overflows containers (fixed-size children larger than a fixed-size
+  parent, negative margins, `Overflow::Visible`, absolute positioning with insets), so
+  the property only holds under so many generator constraints that it would mostly test
+  the generator. The no-gaps rounding property (#7) covers the sound core of this idea
+  (relative positions of siblings) without the unsound bounding claim.
+- **Model rule for `add_child` with an already-attached child.** The first draft of the
+  state machine allowed `add_child`/`insert_child_at_index` to attach a child that
+  already had a parent. This makes `TaffyTree` internally inconsistent (the old parent's
+  `children()` still lists the node while `parent(node)` points at the new parent),
+  unlike `set_children`, which documents and implements detach-from-previous-parent.
+  The docs for `add_child` ("Adds a `child` node under the supplied `parent`") don't
+  say which behaviour is intended, so asserting either way would test an unspecified
+  contract. The committed rules only attach unattached nodes; the discrepancy with
+  `set_children` may still be worth an upstream question, but I did not keep a failing
+  test for behaviour the library never promised.
+- **Exact adjacency for `SpaceBetween`/`SpaceAround`/`SpaceEvenly` justification** was
+  deliberately excluded from property #7 (spacing justifications insert gaps by design)
+  — encoded as generator construction logic rather than a filter.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill + rust reference worked well: 8 of 10 properties compiled and ran on
+the first or second attempt, and both documented setup gotchas I hit (MSRV, `.hegel/`
+gitignore) were accurate. Issues, in decreasing order of importance:
+
+1. **`generators::just` requires `T: Clone + Send + Sync` — undocumented.** The
+   reference presents `just` with no bounds ("Always returns the same value ...
+   `tc.draw(generators::just(42))`"). Taffy's `Dimension`, `LengthPercentage` and
+   `LengthPercentageAuto` wrap a tagged pointer (`*const ()`) and are `!Send + !Sync`,
+   so the natural `one_of!(just(Dimension::AUTO), floats().map(Dimension::from_length))`
+   pattern failed with 12 copies of an opaque error:
+   `error[E0277]: '*const ()' cannot be sent between threads safely ... required by a
+   bound in 'just'`. The fix (draw a `u8` discriminant and construct the value
+   imperatively inside `#[hegel::composite]`) is easy once you know it, but the
+   reference should (a) state the bounds on `just`/`sampled_from`, and (b) show the
+   discriminant-match idiom as the escape hatch for `!Send`/`!Sync` types — layout and
+   arena-style crates hit this class of type routinely. Notably `.map(...)` into a
+   `!Send` type compiled fine, which makes the `just` failure extra surprising.
+
+2. **No documented way to raise `test_cases` globally for the exploratory 10x run.**
+   SKILL.md mandates "Run the suite once with `test_cases` raised ~10x", but the rust
+   reference only shows per-test `#[hegel::test(test_cases = ...)]`. I had to sed-edit
+   every attribute to `test_cases = 1000` and revert afterwards. An env var (like
+   Hypothesis profiles) — or documentation of one if it exists — would make this step
+   one command.
+
+3. **The MSRV gotcha is accurate and saved time.** `cargo add hegeltest` failed exactly
+   as described ("no version of crate `hegeltest` can maintain taffy's rust-version of
+   1.71") and `--ignore-rust-version` fixed it. Good.
+
+4. **The recursive-generator pattern worked verbatim.** The `compose!` +
+   `BoxedGenerator<'static, T>` recipe (including the two fine-print details about the
+   `'static` lifetime and not writing `move |tc|`) compiled first try for `tree_specs`.
+   This section clearly earned its place.
+
+5. **Stateful testing docs were sufficient but two things were discovered by trial:**
+   (a) helper methods on the machine can take `tc: &hegel::TestCase` and call
+   `tc.draw`/`tc.assume` through the reference — the docs only ever show `tc` by value
+   in rules, so I wasn't sure this would work (it does, and it keeps rules readable);
+   (b) failure output prints a nice `Step 1: new_leaf / Step 2: ...` trace — worth
+   advertising in the reference, it made the shrunk machine failures instantly readable.
+
+6. **Draw labels in counterexamples.** Draws bound directly in the test body print with
+   their binding names (`let op = 1;`, `let dirty_idx = 0;`) — excellent. Draws that
+   happen inside composites or through closure factories print as `draw_1 ... draw_6`,
+   which for my six-float min/max test required counting draw order to interpret. A
+   sentence in the reference on when names are preserved (and a suggestion to bind
+   draws to named locals in the test body when readability matters) would help.
+
+7. **Not hegel's fault, but worth a Gotcha:** the failure database re-runs meant the
+   two known failures reproduced instantly on every subsequent `cargo test`, which was
+   great for iterating — the reference mentions `.hegel/` only as a gitignore concern;
+   a sentence saying "failures replay from the database on the next run before new
+   generation" would explain the (initially surprising) instant failures.
+
+8. Everything else — imperative draws, `#[hegel::composite]` with parameters,
+   `sampled_from` for keyword enums, `optional`, bounded `floats::<f32>()` excluding
+   NaN by default, `Verbosity` note that passing tests print nothing — worked exactly
+   as documented.
+
+## Environment notes (not skill issues)
+
+- The default GNU linker was OOM-killed linking the test binaries in this container;
+  builds/runs used `RUSTFLAGS="-Clink-arg=-fuse-ld=lld"` and reduced debuginfo. This
+  affects how to *run* the suite here, not the tests themselves.
